gm-method (a reported visual bug whose known root cause is verified-correct live, yet the user still observes it, and the headless browser harness structurally cannot reproduce the real condition): do NOT declare 'works on my machine' and do NOT keep burning runs against a harness that physically cannot show it. Three structural harness limits block faithful repro of a close-approach gameplay-camera bug: (1) software-GL headless renders the heavy scene at ~1fps, so real movement input integrates almost nothing and the player cannot walk into the forest at speed; (2) a TPS/follow camera rig reclaims camera.position+pitch every frame AND exposes yaw/pitch as GETTER-ONLY (cam.yaw=... is a silent no-op), so a synthetic edit-camera cannot aim without pumping editLook deltas; (3) the local-player state/mesh is often absent from window.__app (a leaner object than the app.js API surface), so there is no handle to deterministically teleport the gameplay player next to a target. When all three bite, the witnessing move is: ship a GATED in-engine diagnostic (window.__xProbe=true records the exact per-frame state -- LOD draw counts, frustum test, camNear, dist -- into window.__x plus a window.__xHits array for the on-screen-but-undrawn event), browser-witness that the probe populates (satisfies the client-edit gate), add a source-shape regression test locking the already-correct invariant, commit+push, and hand the USER precise console capture steps whose single readout distinguishes the live hypotheses (cull = in-frustum drawing 0; near-plane camera-clip = drawing with dist near camNear; depth-occlusion = drawing with terrain in front). The diagnostic plus handoff IS the deliverable; the PRD fix row resolves as blocked-on-user-capture (external: their real full-speed environment is genuinely out of reach). Also: AskUserQuestion to pin the exact symptom is decisive when live screenshots cannot reproduce -- e.g. 'whole tree pops out near' + 'both builds, any fps/speed, when close' reframes the search from a BVH cull hypothesis toward a render-pipeline effect.